INT. LOCKED PENTHOUSE OFFICE - NIGHT

Glass walls over a rain-slick city. A desk the size of a pool table. A bank of five monitors, three dark. On the carpet, a spilled glass of scotch and a pair of reading glasses, one lens cracked.

KENNETH ADLER-MOSS (60s, tycoon, silk shirt open at the throat) sits behind the desk with a phone gripped in both hands. Sweat beads at his hairline. A pistol lies on the blotter, six inches from his right hand. He has not touched it.

Across the desk, in the visitor's chair, a woman. NADINE CRUZ (30s, black coat, patient eyes) rests her chin on folded hands. She hasn't spoken in minutes.

A speakerphone on the desk crackles.

VOICE (V.O.)
Mr. Adler-Moss? Mr. Adler-Moss, it's the front desk. We have police in the lobby.

ADLER-MOSS
Tell them. Tell them to come up.

VOICE (V.O.)
Sir, the elevator is out. They'll take the stairs.

ADLER-MOSS
How many floors?

VOICE (V.O.)
Forty-one, sir.

ADLER-MOSS
Forty-one. Christ.

Nadine unfolds her hands. Adler-Moss flinches. She reaches into her coat, slowly, and pulls out a folded sheet of paper. She slides it across the desk. He doesn't take it.

NADINE
Read it.

ADLER-MOSS
What is it?

NADINE
Your signature.

ADLER-MOSS
I don't sign things I haven't read.

NADINE
You signed this one.

He picks it up with two fingers. Reads. His colour drains. He reads it again.

ADLER-MOSS
This is forged.

NADINE
It's notarised.

ADLER-MOSS
By whom?

NADINE
By a man who's very sorry.

The speakerphone crackles. The lobby voice is shaking now.

VOICE (V.O.)
Sir? Sir, are you there?

NADINE
(to the phone, calm)
He's here.

VOICE (V.O.)
Who is that?

NADINE
The reason you won't need the police.

She lifts her hand, finger hovering over the disconnect key. Adler-Moss lunges for the pistol. Nadine slams the button. Static. The line goes dead.

The pistol is in his hand, shaking. She doesn't move.

ADLER-MOSS
I'll shoot.

NADINE
You won't.

ADLER-MOSS
I will.

NADINE
You can't. It isn't loaded.

He stares at the gun. Checks the chamber. Empty. Checks the magazine. Empty.

NADINE (CONT'D)
I took them out at seven.

ADLER-MOSS
You were here at seven?

NADINE
I've been here since lunch.

Below them, very faintly, boots on concrete. A distant door slamming. Voices. Coming up.

Nadine stands, buttons her coat, and for the first time smiles.
